---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import duties from '../data/towing/duties.json';

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SOURCE OF TRUTH.  Nothing on this page states the law from memory.
// src/data/towing/duties.json is a projection, written by
// scripts/sync-towing-data.mjs from ../DC Towing/data/towing-duties.json, which
// is itself generated by that project's scripts/build_duties.py directly from
// 16 DCMR chapter 4. Every duty is bound to a verbatim anchor in the cited
// section, so an amendment breaks the build instead of quietly misstating the
// law here. To change a duty, change the regulation reading in the node.
//
// THE CPPA FIGURE IS DELIBERATELY CONSERVATIVE (node roadmap p1-t4, resolved).
// D.C. Code § 28-3905(k)(2)(A)(i) — note (k)(2), not (k)(1), which is only about
// WHO may sue — gives treble damages or $1,500 per violation, whichever is
// greater. B26-0174 raises the floor to $3,000 and renumbers the remedies to
// § 28-3905(m)(2)(A), but it is enrolled, awaiting congressional review, and its
// private-action provisions hang on an FY2027 budget applicability clause. The
// vendored D.C. Code mirror is the 2026-05-23 series and cannot see past it.
// So the page says "at least $1,500", which is true now AND stays true if the
// increase has landed. Printing $3,000 and being wrong would tell someone their
// claim is worth double what it is. Understate; never overstate.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

const today = new Date().toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
  month: 'long', day: 'numeric', year: 'numeric',
});

const GROUP_TITLES = {
  'towed-from-private-property': 'If it was towed from a lot without your say-so',
  'any-tow': 'What any tow company owes you',
  'the-truck': 'The truck itself',
  'accident-scene': 'At a crash',
  'licensing': 'Licensing',
};

const byGroup = duties.groupOrder.map((g) => ({
  id: g,
  title: GROUP_TITLES[g] ?? g,
  items: duties.duties.filter((d) => d.group === g),
}));

const publicTowDef = duties.definitions.find((d) => d.term === 'Public tow');

// The two questions the whole page turns on. `key` names the radio group.
const ANSWERS = [
  { v: 'yes', label: 'Yes' },
  { v: 'no', label: 'No' },
  { v: 'unk', label: 'I don\u2019t know' },
];

const tests = [
  {
    n: '01',
    key: 'ticket',
    q: 'Was there a ticket?',
    body: 'A car cannot be towed off private property without your consent unless a police officer or a parking enforcement official issued it a citation — or a police officer directed the tow in an emergency.',
    edge: 'A landlord, a property manager, or the owner of the lot cannot authorize it on their own.',
    cite: '16 DCMR § 406.7',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    key: 'control',
    q: 'Is there a control number?',
    body: 'The company had to get a towing control number from DPW before the tow — and the storage lot has to give you that number when they release the car, along with the legal authority for the removal.',
    edge: 'Even in an emergency they still have to obtain one within two hours.',
    cite: '16 DCMR §§ 406.3, 408.9',
  },
];

// EVERY FIGURE BELOW COMES FROM THE GENERATOR (Wave 3, D-1).
// The receipt checker used to hold 100, 275 and 20 as literals in its script, so
// the one component that states a dollar conclusion — and feeds it into a
// complaint addressed to the regulator — sat outside the drift guard § 09
// advertises. These are parsed from § 408.1 and the sync refuses to run without
// them, so a change to the Director's schedule now breaks this build too.
const rates = duties.rateSchedule.rates;
const money = (n) => '$' + (Number.isInteger(n) ? n : n.toFixed(2));

const caps = [
  ['Standard tow', 'a car at or under 8,000 lb, towed within the District', money(rates.standard_tow)],
  ['Storage', 'per 24 hours, or any part of one', money(rates.storage_per_day)],
  ['Drop fee', 'if you arrive and they stop the tow before removing the car', money(rates.drop_fee)],
];

// Retrieval differs completely depending on who took the car, and the reader
// cannot act until they know which. District tows go to a DPW lot and are
// gated on unpaid tickets; private-lot tows go to the company's own storage
// lot and are governed by the duties above. Fees below are DC DMV's published
// figures, which match 16 DCMR § 408.1.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// OPERATIONAL FACTS — hand-transcribed, NOT generated, and the only things on
// this page nothing checks (Wave 3, D-4). They are also the facts a stranded
// reader needs first, which is the wrong combination, so they carry a date and
// their sources, and § 09 says plainly that they are not covered by the build
// guard the duties are.
//
// The finding proved itself on first check: the hours here said 8:30am–4:30pm
// and DPW publishes 8am–6pm. Someone arriving at 5pm would have been told by
// this page that the lot was shut, and paid another $20 day of storage for it.
const operational = {
  checkedOn: 'September 8, 2026',
  sources: [
    ['DPW — Booting and Impoundment', 'https://dpw.dc.gov/page/booting-and-impoundment'],
    ['DC DMV — Booted/Towed Vehicle Fees', 'https://dmv.dc.gov/book/dmv-fees/bootedtowed-vehicle-fees'],
  ],
};

const districtLot = [
  ['Where it goes', 'Blue Plains, 5001 Shepherd Parkway SW — or Bryant Street, 2115 Bryant St NE. Call first; don’t guess.'],
  ['What to bring', 'Your fee payment receipt, the vehicle registration, and a valid driver’s license. Collecting someone else’s car? You need a notarized statement from the owner.'],
  ['How you pay', 'Card only — Visa or MasterCard. Cash and checks are not accepted. Since December 15, 2025 the District adds a 2.5% service fee to card payments, and since cards are the only way to pay, everyone pays it.'],
  ['Hours', 'Weekdays, 8am to 6pm. Closed weekends, District holidays, and whenever the District government is closed.'],
  ['The catch', 'Every outstanding ticket has to be paid first — including tickets on other plates registered to you. And once you’ve paid the storage, collect the car that day or another day’s storage is added.'],
];

// The first three are the regulation's own figures. Boot removal is NOT — booting
// is not towing, and "boot", "immobilize" and "wheel lock" appear nowhere in
// 16 DCMR chapter 4. It stays because a reader collecting a car needs it, but the
// page no longer claims the regulation is its source (Wave 3, D-3).
const districtFees = [
  ['Tow', money(rates.standard_tow), true],
  ['Tow, oversized vehicle', money(rates.heavy_tow), true],
  ['Storage, per day', money(rates.storage_per_day), true],
  ['Boot removal', '$75', false],
];

const privateLot = [
  ['They must tell you where it is', 'The storage lot address is required on the itemized estimate, and the lot must be in the District.', '16 DCMR §§ 409.2, 405.1'],
  ['They must release it promptly', 'On proof of who you are and that it’s your car, plus payment of the towing and storage charges due — unless the government has placed a hold on it. If you think the amount is wrong, pay it and use § 07; that argument is won on paper afterwards, not at the counter.', '16 DCMR § 408.7'],
  ['They must take a card', 'Cash, insurance draft, certified check, bank check, money order, and at least two major credit cards. Cash-only is a violation.', '16 DCMR § 408.8'],
  ['You can inspect it before paying', 'Refusing you that is a violation in itself.', '16 DCMR § 410.13'],
  ['They must hand you five things', 'The towing control number, the legal authority for the removal, an itemized statement, a receipt, and a copy of the Owner’s Bill of Rights.', '16 DCMR § 408.9'],
];

// B-1, Wave 4. This section had FOUR routes and ZERO links — an email that was not
// a mailto, a phone number that was not a tel, a form that was named but not
// linked. Meanwhile the dispatch number in § 01 is tappable twice. The page made
// finding your car actionable and complaining about it not, for the one reader
// whose defining trait is deciding whether it is worth the bother.
//
// Every URL below was checked at its source on the date in `operational`, and
// they are operational facts like the lot addresses — not generated, not covered
// by the build guard. See § 09.
const routes = [
  {
    who: 'DLCP — Department of Licensing and Consumer Protection',
    what: 'The regulator. It is obliged to investigate a complaint, and it can suspend or revoke the license of a towing business or a storage lot. It asks you to file within three years of the incident, and says it opens investigations where losses total $250 or more — or where there is a pattern or practice of abuse. A single tow may fall under that figure. The pattern is made of single tows.',
    links: [
      ['Consumer Protection Unit complaint form', 'https://dcra.kustomer.help/contact/consumer-complaint-form-Sk6BW94Lu'],
      ['(202) 671-4500', 'tel:+12026714500'],
      ['dlcp@dc.gov', 'mailto:dlcp@dc.gov'],
    ],
    cite: '16 DCMR §§ 411.2, 411.4',
  },
  {
    who: 'Check the license first — SCOUT',
    what: 'Every towing business, storage lot and truck in the District has to be licensed. SCOUT will tell you whether this one is. Operating without a license can carry arrest, not just a fine.',
    links: [['scout.dlcp.dc.gov', 'https://scout.dlcp.dc.gov']],
    cite: '16 DCMR §§ 402.1, 410.1, 411.8',
  },
  {
    who: 'The Office of the Attorney General',
    what: 'OAG enforces the Consumer Protection Procedures Act and can sue a company for restitution and penalties. Its Consumer and Tenant Response line will also mediate a dispute, though it does not represent you.',
    links: [
      ['OAG consumer complaint form', 'https://dcoag.my.site.com/dcoagcomplaints/s/?language=en_US'],
      ['(202) 442-9828', 'tel:+12024429828'],
    ],
    cite: 'D.C. Code §§ 28-3901–3913',
  },
  {
    who: 'You, directly',
    what: 'The CPPA lets you sue directly, and recover treble damages or at least $1,500 per violation — whichever is greater — plus your attorney’s fees, punitive damages and an injunction. This is not a sum you can compute from the list above: a court decides what counts as a violation and how many there were. Small claims in D.C. Superior Court is a realistic venue.',
    links: [['Superior Court Small Claims Branch', 'https://www.dccourts.gov/superior-court/superior-court-divisions/civil-division/small-claims']],
    how: 'Small Claims handles money claims up to $10,000, which is well above what a towing dispute is likely to be worth. A consumer lawyer is the other route.',
    cite: 'D.C. Code § 28-3905(k)(2)',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Towed in DC? Find your car, and find out if it was legal"
  description="Your car is gone in Washington DC. Call (202) 541-6083 to find out where it is. Then: a tow you did not agree to is a public tow, which means it needed a citation and a DPW control number, and it is capped at $100 plus $20 a day. What the tow company or the District owes you under 16 DCMR chapter 4, how to get the car back, and who to complain to. No account, no AI, nothing leaves your browser."
>
  <Header slot="header" />

  <svg width="0" height="0" style="position:absolute" aria-hidden="true">
    <symbol id="ips-star" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><polygon points="12,1 14.5,8.5 22,8.5 16,13 18,21 12,16.5 6,21 8,13 2,8.5 9.5,8.5"/></symbol>
  </svg>

  <!-- LEAD -->
  <section class="ips-lead">
    <div class="ps-container">
      <p class="ps-record ps-record--red ips-dateline">{today.toUpperCase()} &nbsp;|&nbsp; AFTER A TOW</p>
      <div class="ips-lead__grid">
        <div class="ips-lead__main">
          <h1 class="ips-lead__h">Your car is <span class="ps-highlight">gone</span>. Two questions.</h1>

          <!-- A-1, Wave 1. The dispatch number was 1.44 phone screens down, behind sixty words
               of orientation and a decorative receipt. Whether the car was towed or stolen is the
               only question the reader has in the first minute, so the number comes before the
               explaining. It appears again in § 01 with its context; that repetition is deliberate
               and it is the one fact on this page worth saying twice. -->
          <p class="ips-lead__first">
            <span class="ps-record ips-lead__first-k">First — towed, or stolen?</span>
            <a class="ips-lead__tel" href="tel:+12025416083">(202) 541-6083</a>
            <span class="ips-lead__first-n">Towing Control Dispatch. They can tell you whether it
              was towed and which lot it went to.</span>
          </p>

          <p class="ips-lead__blurb">
            Whoever took it — the District, or a private lot — <strong>you did not consent to it, and the
            District calls that a public tow.</strong> Public tows come with rules that had to be followed
            <em>before</em> the truck arrived, a published ceiling on what you can be charged, and things
            they owe you when you turn up to collect. Most people are never told any of it.
          </p>
          <p class="ips-lead__note ps-record">
            16 DCMR chapter 4 &middot; not legal advice &middot; this page states the rule, not the merits of your tow
          </p>
        </div>
        <aside class="ips-lead__rail">
          <div class="tw-stub" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="tw-stub__bars"></span>
            <p class="tw-stub__h">Public tow</p>
            <p class="tw-stub__sub">16 DCMR &sect; 408.1 &middot; maximum</p>
            <dl class="tw-stub__lines">
              <div class="tw-stub__line"><dt>Tow</dt><dd>100.00</dd></div>
              <div class="tw-stub__line"><dt>Storage / day</dt><dd>20.00</dd></div>
              <div class="tw-stub__line"><dt>Drop fee</dt><dd>50.00</dd></div>
            </dl>
            <p class="tw-stub__ask">Anything else on it?</p>
            <p class="tw-stub__ans">Ask why.</p>
          </div>
          <p class="rk-privacy ps-record">
            No account. No AI. Nothing you read here leaves your browser. Every duty below is generated
            straight from the regulation — see the method note at the end.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- §01 FIND IT FIRST -->
  <section class="ips-sec" id="find-it">
    <div class="ps-container">
      <div class="ips-sec-head">
        <span class="ps-record ps-record--red">&sect; 01</span>
        <h2 class="ips-sec-title">Find it first</h2>
        <span class="ips-sec-rule"></span>
      </div>

      <p class="ips-lede">
        Before anything else — <strong>is it towed, or is it stolen?</strong> Most people can’t tell,
        and the answer changes what you do next. There is one number that settles it.
      </p>

      <div class="tw-call">
        <p class="ps-record tw-call__label">Towing Control Dispatch Center</p>
        <p class="tw-call__num"><a href="tel:+12025416083">(202) 541-6083</a></p>
        <p class="tw-call__note">
          They can tell you whether your car was towed and which lot it went to. If they have no
          record of it, treat it as stolen and report it to the police.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="tw-note">
        If the District towed it, the government is also supposed to notify the registered owner of
        the tow and where the car is. <span class="tw-cite ps-record">16 DCMR &sect; 406.10</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- §01 THE TWO QUESTIONS -->
  <section class="ips-sec" id="two-questions">
    <div class="ps-container">
      <div class="ips-sec-head">
        <span class="ps-record ps-record--red">&sect; 02</span>
        <h2 class="ips-sec-title">Ask these two things</h2>
        <span class="ips-sec-rule"></span>
      </div>

      <p class="tw-def">
        <span class="ps-record ps-record--red tw-def__label">The definition it all turns on</span>
        A <strong>private tow</strong> is one you asked for. A <strong>public tow</strong> is one you didn’t —
        and the regulation says so in as many words, listing
        <span class="ps-mark-und">tows from private real property</span> among them.
        That single line is why the rules below apply to a tow out of your own apartment lot.
        <span class="tw-cite ps-record">{publicTowDef?.citation ?? '16 DCMR § 499'}</span>
      </p>

      <ol class="tw-tests">
        {tests.map((t) => (
          <li class="tw-test">
            <p class="tw-test__n ps-record ps-record--red">{t.n}</p>
            <div class="tw-test__body">
              <h3 class="tw-test__q">{t.q}</h3>
              <p class="tw-test__text">{t.body}</p>
              <p class="tw-test__edge"><strong>{t.edge}</strong></p>
              <fieldset class="tw-ask">
                <legend class="ps-record tw-ask__legend">Your answer</legend>
                <div class="tw-ask__opts">
                  {ANSWERS.map((a) => (
                    <span class="tw-ask__opt">
                      <input class="tw-ask__in" type="radio" data-q={t.key}
                             id={`tw-${t.key}-${a.v}`} name={`tw-${t.key}`} value={a.v} />
                      <label class="tw-ask__lab" for={`tw-${t.key}-${a.v}`}>{a.label}</label>
                    </span>
                  ))}
                </div>
              </fieldset>
              <p class="tw-cite ps-record">{t.cite}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      <div class="tw-stand" id="twStand" role="status" aria-live="polite">
        <p class="ps-record tw-stand__label">Where you stand</p>
        <p class="tw-stand__verdict" id="twVerdict">
          Answer both and your position appears here. Nothing you tap leaves this page.
        </p>
        <p class="tw-stand__detail" id="twDetail"></p>
      </div>

      <p class="tw-note">
        This tells you what the regulation required of them. <strong>It does not rule on your tow</strong> —
        only DLCP, or a court, can do that.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- §02 WHAT IT CAN COST -->
  <section class="ips-sec" id="what-it-costs">
    <div class="ps-container">
      <div class="ips-sec-head">
        <span class="ps-record ps-record--red">&sect; 03</span>
        <h2 class="ips-sec-title">What it can cost</h2>
        <span class="ips-sec-rule"></span>
      </div>

      <p class="ips-lede">
        Because it’s a public tow, the District’s own published schedule sets the ceiling — not the rates
        the company advertises, and not the rates painted on the truck. Those govern tows you
        <em>request</em>.
      </p>

      <table class="tw-caps">
        <caption class="ps-record">Maximum public-tow charges &middot; 16 DCMR &sect; 408.1</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Charge</th><th scope="col">What it covers</th><th scope="col" class="tw-caps__num">Cap</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {caps.map(([name, what, amount]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{name}</th>
              <td>{what}</td>
              <td class="tw-caps__num"><strong>{amount}</strong></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="tw-rc" data-storage-per-day={rates.storage_per_day}
           data-road-service={rates.road_service}
           data-mileage-standard={rates.mileage_standard}
           data-mileage-heavy={rates.mileage_heavy}>
        <p class="ps-record tw-rc__label">Check it against your receipt</p>
        <div class="tw-rc__row">
          <p class="tw-rc__field">
            <label for="twCharged">What they charged you</label>
            <span class="tw-rc__money">
              <span class="tw-rc__cur" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
              <input type="number" id="twCharged" min="0" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="0.00" />
            </span>
          </p>
          <p class="tw-rc__field">
            <label for="twDays">Days it sat in the lot</label>
            <input type="number" id="twDays" min="0" max="365" step="1" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="0" />
          </p>
          <p class="tw-rc__field">
            <label for="twSize">The vehicle</label>
            <select id="twSize">
              <option value={rates.standard_tow}>Car or light truck, 8,000 lb or under</option>
              <option value={rates.heavy_tow}>Heavy, over 8,000 lb</option>
            </select>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tw-rc__out" id="twRcOut" role="status" aria-live="polite">
          <p class="tw-rc__verdict" id="twRcVerdict">Enter what you paid and the lawful ceiling appears here.</p>
          <p class="tw-rc__detail" id="twRcDetail"></p>
        </div>
        <p class="tw-rc__foot">
          <strong>The ceiling above is the tow plus storage, and nothing else.</strong> &sect; 408.1
          also allows road service and a per-mile charge beyond the District line, and neither is in
          the figure — so a receipt that includes one of those can be lawful and still read as over.
          Check the itemized statement before you rely on the difference.
        </p>
        <p class="tw-rc__foot">
          This is the <strong>public-tow</strong> schedule — the one that applies when you didn’t agree to the
          tow. A tow you called for yourself is a private tow and has <em>no</em> published ceiling; what
          protects you there is the signed, itemized estimate they owe you <em>before</em> the tow begins.
          <span class="tw-cite ps-record">16 DCMR &sect;&sect; 408.1, 409.1</span>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="tw-note">
        A heavy vehicle — over 8,000 lb — is capped at $275 instead. That larger figure circulates widely as
        “the DC tow fee.” For an ordinary car it is <span class="ps-mark-und">not the number</span>.
      </p>
      <p class="tw-note">
        Charging above the schedule for a public tow is itself prohibited, and the infraction schedule
        treats it as Class 1. <span class="tw-cite ps-record">16 DCMR &sect;&sect; 410.12, 3304.1(l)</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- §03 THE DUTIES -->
  <section class="ips-sec" id="duties">
    <div class="ps-container">
      <div class="ips-sec-head">
        <span class="ps-record ps-record--red">&sect; 04</span>
        <h2 class="ips-sec-title">What they owe you</h2>
        <span class="ips-sec-rule"></span>
      </div>

      <p class="ips-lede">
        Every line below is generated from the regulation itself. Where the District’s infraction schedule
        grades a violation, the class is shown — <strong>Class 1 is the most serious</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- BLIND-1, Wave 4. Measured: every statement of the delay warning lived only in the
           JavaScript bundle and fired only after the reader answered a question or typed a
           figure. Zero static occurrences. So a reader who scrolled met twenty-three infraction
           badges and nothing about the clock — the project's core standing lens satisfied only
           for people who interact. This is that sentence, static, immediately above the longest
           block of "they broke the rule" content on the page. -->
      <p class="tw-clock">
        <strong>A broken rule is not a reason to leave the car there.</strong> Storage runs at
        $20 a day and it keeps running while a complaint is decided, which takes weeks. Nothing
        below is a reason to refuse payment or to argue at the counter — the way to contest a tow
        is to pay it, collect the car, keep the itemized receipt, and use &sect; 06.
      </p>

      {byGroup.map((g) => (
        <div class="tw-group">
          <h3 class="tw-group__h">{g.title}</h3>
          <ul class="tw-duties">
            {g.items.map((d) => (
              <li class="tw-duty">
                <p class="tw-duty__text">{d.plain}</p>
                <p class="tw-duty__meta ps-record">
                  <span class="tw-cite">{d.citation}</span>
                  {d.infractionClass && (
                    <span class={`tw-class tw-class--${d.infractionClass.replace(/\s+/g, '').toLowerCase()}`}>
                      {d.infractionClass} infraction
                      {d.infractionClassVia && <span class="tw-class__via"> (scheduled under {d.infractionClassVia})</span>}
                    </span>
                  )}
                </p>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- §05 GETTING IT BACK -->
  <section class="ips-sec" id="getting-it-back">
    <div class="ps-container">
      <div class="ips-sec-head">
        <span class="ps-record ps-record--red">&sect; 05</span>
        <h2 class="ips-sec-title">Getting it back</h2>
        <span class="ips-sec-rule"></span>
      </div>

      <p class="ips-lede">
        This is where the two kinds of tow stop behaving the same way. <strong>Who took it decides
        where it is, what you pay, and what they owe you.</strong>
      </p>

      <div class="tw-fork">
        <div class="tw-fork__col">
          <h3 class="tw-fork__h">The District towed it</h3>
          <p class="tw-fork__when ps-record">Unpaid tickets, a snow emergency, illegal parking</p>
          <dl class="tw-dl">
            {districtLot.map(([k, v]) => (<><dt>{k}</dt><dd>{v}</dd></>))}
          </dl>
          <table class="tw-caps tw-caps--tight">
            <caption class="ps-record">DC DMV published fees</caption>
            <tbody>
              {districtFees.map(([k, v]) => (
                <tr><th scope="row">{k}</th><td class="tw-caps__num"><strong>{v}</strong></td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="tw-note">
            The tow and storage figures are the regulation’s own public-tow schedule.
            <span class="tw-cite ps-record">16 DCMR &sect; 408.1</span>
          </p>
          <p class="tw-note tw-opnote">
            The five facts above — the lots, the hours, what to bring, how you pay — are
            <strong>transcribed by hand from DPW and DC DMV, not generated</strong>, so nothing in
            this page’s build would catch it if they changed. Checked {operational.checkedOn}
            against {operational.sources.map(([n, u], i) => (<>{i > 0 ? ' and ' : ''}<a href={u} rel="noopener noreferrer">{n}</a></>))}.
            Call ahead anyway; a phone call beats any web page, including this one.
          </p>
          <p class="tw-note">
            <strong>Boot removal is the exception.</strong> Booting is not towing, and chapter 4 does
            not reach it — that $75 is a DMV fee for taking a wheel clamp off, not a capped towing
            charge, and nothing on this page governs it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tw-fork__col">
          <h3 class="tw-fork__h">A private lot towed it</h3>
          <p class="tw-fork__when ps-record">An apartment lot, a retail lot, a space you were parked in</p>
          <ul class="tw-duties">
            {privateLot.map(([k, v, cite]) => (
              <li class="tw-duty">
                <p class="tw-duty__text"><strong>{k}.</strong> {v}</p>
                <p class="tw-duty__meta ps-record"><span class="tw-cite">{cite}</span></p>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p class="tw-note">
            And the question from &sect; 02 still applies: <strong>was there a citation?</strong> If
            not, they should not have taken it at all.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- §04 WHAT TO DO -->
  <section class="ips-sec" id="what-to-do">
    <div class="ps-container">
      <div class="ips-sec-head">
        <span class="ps-record ps-record--red">&sect; 06</span>
        <h2 class="ips-sec-title">What to do now</h2>
        <span class="ips-sec-rule"></span>
      </div>

      <p class="ips-lede">
        Two different moments, and only the first one is urgent. Almost nothing here has to happen
        tonight.
      </p>

      <div class="tw-when">
        <div class="tw-when__col">
          <h3 class="tw-when__h">If the truck is still there</h3>
          <p class="tw-when__sub ps-record">Minutes matter here, and only here</p>
          <ol class="tw-first">
            <li><strong>You can ask them to stop.</strong> If they have hooked your car up but have
              not driven off, they have to discontinue the tow once you pay the rate set for a
              discontinued tow — and, where an officer ordered it and is still on the scene, with
              their agreement. <span class="tw-cite ps-record">16 DCMR &sect; 408.6</span></li>
            <li><strong>Photograph the truck.</strong> Both cab doors and both sides. The name,
              address and phone have to be there, and so does the truck’s own ID number.
              <span class="tw-cite ps-record">16 DCMR &sect;&sect; 404.2, 404.4</span></li>
          </ol>
          <p class="tw-safety">
            <strong>You don’t have to win the argument.</strong> Photographs work from a distance,
            and the regulation binds the company rather than the person in the cab — a complaint is
            decided on the paperwork, not on what was said in the lot. If you feel unsafe, leave.
            Apart from the two steps above, nothing on this page has to happen tonight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tw-when__col">
          <h3 class="tw-when__h">When you collect the car</h3>
          <p class="tw-when__sub ps-record">At the counter, in daylight, with a pen</p>
          <ol class="tw-first">
            <li><strong>Ask for the towing control number.</strong> They have to give it to you. If
              there isn’t one, that is the answer to question two.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask what citation authorized the tow</strong>, and who requested it. The
              company had to report both to DPW before towing.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask to inspect the car before you pay.</strong> Refusing that is a violation
              in itself.</li>
            <li><strong>Get the itemized receipt.</strong> They cannot refuse it, and they have to
              accept a card — cash-only is not lawful.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- B-3, Wave 4. The routes come before the packet, so a reader met the addresses
           before they had anything to send, and § 06 had no forward pointer at all. -->
      <p class="tw-note tw-forward">
        <strong>Before you send anything, look at &sect; 07.</strong> It assembles what you have
        already worked out on this page — the two answers, the receipt comparison and the citations
        — into a complaint you can copy or print. It is easier to send one thing once.
      </p>

      <div class="tw-routes">
        {routes.map((r) => (
          <div class="tw-route">
            <h3 class="tw-route__who">{r.who}</h3>
            <p class="tw-route__what">{r.what}</p>
            {r.how && <p class="tw-route__what">{r.how}</p>}
            <p class="tw-route__how ps-record">
              {r.links.map(([label, href], i) => (
                <>{i > 0 ? ' · ' : ''}<a href={href} rel="noopener noreferrer">{label}</a></>
              ))}
            </p>
            <p class="tw-cite ps-record">{r.cite}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- §07 THE PACKET -->
  <section class="ips-sec" id="packet">
    <div class="ps-container">
      <div class="ips-sec-head">
        <span class="ps-record ps-record--red">&sect; 07</span>
        <h2 class="ips-sec-title">Put it in writing</h2>
        <span class="ips-sec-rule"></span>
      </div>

      <p class="ips-lede">
        You have answered the two questions and checked the receipt. <strong>That is most of a
        complaint already.</strong> Fill in the four things only you know and this assembles the
        rest — the figures, the citations, and what DLCP is obliged to do with it.
      </p>

      <p class="tw-note">
        It is built in this browser and never sent anywhere. Copy it, print it, edit it — it is
        yours, and it will read better in your own words than in ours.
      </p>

      <div class="tw-pk">
        <div class="tw-pk__row">
          <p class="tw-pk__field">
            <label for="pkDate">Date of the tow</label>
            <input type="date" id="pkDate" />
          </p>
          <p class="tw-pk__field">
            <label for="pkFrom">Where it was taken from</label>
            <input type="text" id="pkFrom" placeholder="street address or lot" autocomplete="off" />
          </p>
          <p class="tw-pk__field">
            <label for="pkCo">Company named on your receipt</label>
            <input type="text" id="pkCo" placeholder="as printed" autocomplete="off" />
          </p>
          <p class="tw-pk__field">
            <label for="pkLot">Storage lot you collected from</label>
            <input type="text" id="pkLot" placeholder="address, if you have it" autocomplete="off" />
          </p>
          <p class="tw-pk__field">
            <label for="pkScout">Did SCOUT list that company as licensed?</label>
            <select id="pkScout">
              <option value="unchecked">I have not checked yet</option>
              <option value="listed">Yes, it was listed</option>
              <option value="absent">No, I could not find it</option>
            </select>
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="tw-pk__scout">
          Check it yourself at <a href="https://scout.dlcp.dc.gov" rel="noopener noreferrer">scout.dlcp.dc.gov</a>
          and note the date you looked. A check you ran on a date you can state is worth more than
          anything this page could tell you, because a license status changes and yours is the one
          that was true when you looked.
          <span class="tw-cite ps-record">16 DCMR &sect;&sect; 402.1, 410.1</span>
        </p>

        <p class="tw-pk__actions">
          <button type="button" id="pkBuild" class="tw-pk__btn tw-pk__btn--go">Build my complaint</button>
          <button type="button" id="pkCopy" class="tw-pk__btn" hidden>Copy it</button>
          <button type="button" id="pkPrint" class="tw-pk__btn" hidden>Print it</button>
        </p>

        <p class="tw-pk__status ps-record" id="pkStatus" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>

        <label for="pkOut" class="tw-pk__outlabel">Your complaint</label>
        <textarea id="pkOut" class="tw-pk__out" rows="24" spellcheck="true" hidden></textarea>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- §08 THE OTHER SIDE.  C-1, Wave 2. Before this the property manager appeared on the page
       exactly twice, both times as a prohibition, and never once as someone with a lawful option.
       The framework's warning for axis C is that a page which only arms one side gets dismissed by
       the side that actually orders the tows. Placed after the resident's arc completes rather than
       inside it, so it interrupts nobody and is still findable by someone sent the link. -->
  <section class="ips-sec" id="if-you-ordered-it">
    <div class="ps-container">
      <div class="ips-sec-head">
        <span class="ps-record ps-record--red">&sect; 08</span>
        <h2 class="ips-sec-title">If you manage the property</h2>
        <span class="ips-sec-rule"></span>
      </div>

      <p class="ips-lede">
        Everything above is written for the person whose car was taken. This part is for the person
        who called the truck — <strong>because the rule that protects them is the same rule that
        binds you</strong>, and it is easy to get wrong in good faith.
      </p>

      <div class="tw-mgr">
        <p>
          <strong>The problem is real.</strong> Someone blocks the dumpster on collection day.
          Someone takes a resident’s space and does it again the following week. You have a lot to
          run and no obvious way to clear it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>But you cannot authorize the tow yourself.</strong> A vehicle may not be towed
          from private real property without the vehicle owner’s consent unless a police officer or
          a parking enforcement official has issued it a citation, or a police officer directs the
          tow in an emergency. Owning or managing the lot does not substitute for that.
          <span class="tw-cite ps-record">16 DCMR &sect; 406.7</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>So the lawful route runs through a citation.</strong> Get the vehicle cited first;
          the tow can follow. A company that tells you a citation is unnecessary is telling you
          something the regulation does not say.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And your name is on the record either way.</strong> Before a tow from private real
          property, the towing business has to give DPW the name, address and phone number of the
          person who requested it, along with the authority they were acting under. Every tow you
          order is logged with the District against you by name — which is worth knowing before you
          rely on anyone’s assurance about what is allowed.
          <span class="tw-cite ps-record">16 DCMR &sect; 406.4</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The gap is not one you made.</strong> The District used to have a statutory
          procedure for vehicles left on private property — D.C. Code &sect;&sect; 50-2621 to
          50-2624, which &sect; 406.7 still points at today. Those sections were repealed in
          <strong>October 2003</strong>, fourteen months before this chapter was published citing
          them, and were never replaced. There is no posted-sign-and-notice regime in the chapter to
          fall back on. One route was left standing, and it runs through a government official.
        </p>
        <p class="tw-mgr__ask">
          <strong>One thing this page cannot tell you.</strong> Whether parking enforcement will
          actually come out to private property to write that citation. We do not know, and it is
          the question the whole subject turns on. <span class="ps-mark-und">If you have tried it,
          we would genuinely like to know what happened</span> — there is a form at the foot of this
          page, and it goes to a person, not a database.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- METHOD -->
  <section class="ips-sec" id="method">
    <div class="ps-container">
      <div class="ips-sec-head">
        <span class="ps-record ps-record--red">&sect; 09</span>
        <h2 class="ips-sec-title">How this page knows</h2>
        <span class="ips-sec-rule"></span>
      </div>
      <div class="dc-sources">
        <p>
          The duties above are not transcribed by hand. They are generated from
          <strong>{duties.chapter}</strong>, and each one is bound to a verbatim phrase that has to still
          appear in the section it cites — if the District amends the chapter, this page fails to build
          rather than telling you something that stopped being true.
        </p>
        <p>
          The chapter’s currency was checked against the District’s own register of rulemakings. The
          prohibition on towing from private property without a citation has one rulemaking on record and
          has never been amended.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Two kinds of fact live on this page, and only one of them is generated.</strong>
          The duties, the infraction classes and the rate schedule are read from the regulation and
          the build stops if they drift. The <em>operational</em> details — the dispatch number, the
          lot addresses and hours, what to bring, the agency phone numbers — are transcribed by hand
          from DPW and DC DMV. Nothing here checks those, so they carry the date they were last
          checked, in &sect; 05. They are also the facts you need first, which is exactly the wrong
          combination, and it is why every one of them says to call ahead.
        </p>
        <p class="ps-record tw-asof">
          Generated from the regulation on {duties.nodeGeneratedAt?.slice(0, 10) ?? 'the date shown above'} &middot;
          {' '}{duties.duties.length} duties &middot; sources: 16 DCMR chapter 4, D.C. Official Code
        </p>
        <!-- C-2, Wave 2. The standing lens: this caveat is ours to state, not theirs to find.
             Framed as arming the reader rather than unsettling them, because a resident who reads
             "the enabling statute was repealed" and stops there has been harmed by us. The
             narrower price-control question is kept separate and hedged, because it is derived
             and untested — and since the receipt-checker now computes against § 408.1, it is
             load-bearing in a way it was not before. -->
        <div class="tw-obj">
          <h3 class="tw-obj__h">The objection an operator may raise</h3>
          <p>
            If you take this page to a tow company, this is the argument you might get back. It is a
            real one, so it is worth knowing it and knowing the answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Chapter 4’s enabling act was repealed.</strong> D.C. Code &sect; 47-2850, the Towing
            Regulation and Enforcement Authority, was repealed by the BEST Act of 2022 with effect from
            October 1, 2025, and that act carries no savings clause.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The chapter stands anyway, on three grounds.</strong> The District’s Office of
            Documents still publishes it as in force, and a regulation stays effective until it is
            itself amended or repealed. The licensing and enforcement machinery has live replacement
            authority in <strong>D.C. Code &sect; 47-2851.01b</strong>, which directs the
            Director to issue rules and lets the Department suspend or revoke any license under that
            subchapter — and towing sits inside it, because “vehicular services” is a license category
            the statute defines to include towing. And DLCP, the agency that administers all of this,
            published towing guidance for residents on <strong>June 30, 2026</strong>, nine months after
            the repeal took effect.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>One narrower question is genuinely open</strong>, and it is not the one above.
            &sect; 47-2851.01b authorizes rules to implement a subchapter about <em>licensing</em>.
            Whether that also carries a <span class="ps-mark-und">price control</span> — the rate
            schedule in &sect; 408.1, the ceiling this page checks receipts against — is a narrower
            question than whether chapter 4 survives, and this page has not found a decision settling
            it either way. Two things are worth knowing while it is open: those figures are also
            <strong>DC DMV’s own published fees</strong>, and DLCP’s own guidance tells residents to
            complain when they are charged too much for a tow.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          <strong>This is not legal advice</strong>, and it does not tell you whether your own tow was lawful —
          only what the rule requires. If a tow company disagrees with something here, the regulation is
          public and the citations are on every line.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer slot="footer" />
</Base>

